Direct presidential elections were held for the first time in [[Cape Verde]] on 17 February 1991, as previously the [[National Assembly of Cape Verde|National Assembly]] had elected the President. The result was a victory for [[António Mascarenhas Monteiro]] of the [[Movement for Democracy (Cape Verde)|Movement for Democracy]], which had also won the [[1991 Cape Verdean parliamentary election|parliamentary elections]] the previous month. He defeated incumbent [[Aristides Pereira]] of the [[African Party for the Independence of Cape Verde]].<ref>[http://africanelections.tripod.com/cv.html Elections in Cape Verde] {{Webarchive|url=https://web.archive.org/web/20110717190543/http://africanelections.tripod.com/cv.html |date=2011-07-17 }} African Elections Database</ref> Voter turnout was 61.4%.<ref>[[Dieter Nohlen]], Michael Krennerich & Bernhard Thibaut (1999) ''Elections in Africa: A data handbook'', p201 {{ISBN|0-19-829645-2}}</ref>

Monteiro took office on 22 March.

